GroovyMAME nogpu: optional kernel pacing of each frame (-mister_pacing <factor>)

GroovyMAME hands every frame to the socket in one go and the kernel sends it as one
wire-speed burst. A receiver that races the beam with a small line buffer (Groovy_TaNGer:
32 lines of BRAM on a Tang Primer 20K) is outrun: in RGB565 a 320-px line takes 53 us on
a 100 Mbit wire against 63.6 us per 15 kHz raster line, the writer gains ~40 lines a
frame and overwrites lines not yet shown.

With -mister_pacing F (F > 0), each CMD_SWITCHRES sets SO_MAX_PACING_RATE on the socket
to F x (bytes per raster line incl. 42 bytes of Ethernet/IPv4/UDP headers per 1472-byte
datagram) x hfreq. sendto() still returns at once - the emulation thread never waits,
frame delay is untouched - and the kernel's fq qdisc releases the datagrams at the
raster's line rate. Default 0 = off (unchanged behaviour). Suggested F: 1.0 to 1.02.

Requirements (Linux only; elsewhere the option is ignored with a verbose message):
  - the interface must use the fq qdisc (fq_codel, systemd's default, ignores the rate):
      tc qdisc replace dev <if> root fq flow_limit 1000
    fq's default flow_limit (100 packets per flow) is less than one frame (105 datagrams
    for 320x240 RGB565, 126 for 384x240): the tail of every frame would be dropped.
  - the socket send buffer must hold a frame while it is paced out: GroovyMAME asks for
    2 MB, which needs net.core.wmem_max >= 2 MB (4 MB on Arch/GroovyArcade).

Depends on: groovymame-mister-rgb565.patch (applied first; m_bpp comes from it).
Base: substring/GroovyMAME 4ce01b9 (libretro-driver) + the rgb565 patch. Apply with
  git apply -p1 groovymame-mister-pacing.patch
Checked: applies; drawnogpu.cpp and osdobj_common.cpp compile (g++ -std=c++20 -Werror
-fsyntax-only, SO_MAX_PACING_RATE defined). Not yet run against a receiver.

